## Large-schema stress harness for the staged Generate flow (issue #173)
================================================================================

Context: PR #206 review asked for a live Generate on a wide table before
#173 can close, and the author has no Foundation Model access. Re-reading
the flow also showed the PR's wide-table claim was too broad: Stage-3
completion only receives the closed entity catalog (ids, labels,
descriptions, prior relations) — never table columns — so paging fixes
"too many entities x items for one answer", while the wide-table and
100-table reports most plausibly fail in Stage-1 detection, the only stage
that reads metadata. A one-command live harness lets a maintainer with FM
access locate the failing stage instead of guessing.

Changes:

1. `tests/eval/large_schema_stress.py` (new)
   Runs the real `detect_entities` -> `infer_relations` ->
   `infer_attributes` -> `infer_axioms` chain (checkpointed like
   `GenerateWorkflow.run_completion`) on synthetic schemas shaped like the
   #173 reports: `wide_table_164`, `wide_table_360`, `many_tables_100x18`.
   The LLM call is only observed (wrapped, never altered): per stage it
   reports items, LLM calls, truncated calls, largest request size, message
   count and token usage, plus the failing stage, raw error and the
   user-visible message. `detection_visibility` measures, with the real
   `get_metadata` tool, how much of the schema the Stage-1 overview shows.
   `--mlflow-experiment` logs metrics + the JSON report to one MLflow run
   and prints its URI; exit code is non-zero on any failed scenario. Host
   and token are never logged.
2. `tests/eval/test_large_schema_stress.py` (new)
   Offline coverage with a scripted LLM: schema shapes, the visibility
   caps, a full chain observing 3 attribute pages in single
   `[system, user]` requests, and failure-stage / user-message reporting.
3. `tests/eval/README.md`
   "Large-schema stress run" section with the live command.

Measured offline (real `get_metadata` tool, no LLM): the Stage-1 overview
shows 80/164 and 80/360 columns of a single wide table, and 50/100 tables
(900/1800 columns, an ~87k-char tool result) for the 100-table schema; the
rest is only reachable if the model calls `get_table_detail`.

Files modified:
- tests/eval/large_schema_stress.py (new)
- tests/eval/test_large_schema_stress.py (new)
- tests/eval/README.md

Tests (frozen env):
- pytest tests/eval -q -> 45 passed (5 new).

